  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. Multiple records use molecular-dynamics tensile loading at comparable strain rates and temperatures on the closely related FCC CoNiCrFeMn/Cantor-type alloy, while others supply an additional 10^10 s^-1 rate. They repeatedly connect yielding and stress drops with partial-dislocation nucleation, stacking faults, local HCP coordination, and thermally facilitated nucleation or reduced strength. Thus an expert could plausibly propose a bulk FeNiCrMn single-crystal MD study varying temperature, strain rate, and loading direction. Important details are not directly anticipated—especially vacancy-cluster nucleation, the precise crystallographic directions, rate-insensitive yield strength, and the absence of twinning or bulk FCC-to-HCP transformation; indeed, several close records instead predict TWIP/TRIP. These gaps limit exact anticipation but do not require a substantial new intervention concept."
